# The build context is excluded in full, and then one file is admitted back by
# name. Nothing else is sent to the daemon: not the .git directory, not a data
# file that strayed into the tree, not the repository's own code, which arrives
# through the /work bind mount rather than through a COPY.
#
# The exclusion is not cosmetic. A build context is uploaded before the first
# instruction runs, so a file that must never leave the machine would leave it
# here, before any rule in the Dockerfile could apply.
#
# The one exception is the caller in implementation/tools/, which quality/
# REF-011 decision 2 requires to be compiled in a builder stage and copied into
# the runtime, so its source has to reach the daemon. It is admitted by its
# exact path and not by a pattern: "!implementation/tools/*.c" would admit a
# file nobody has read yet, and the point of excluding everything is that what
# is sent is a list somebody wrote down.
*
!implementation/tools/iso-extract.c
